  "latest_nl_proof": "Let n>1 be an integer, let m be an integer with 0<=m<=n-1, and let t be a real number with m/n <= t < (m+1)/n. We prove that floor(n t) <= m.\n\nSince n>1, we have n>0. Multiplying the inequality t < (m+1)/n by the positive number n gives n t < m+1.\n\nBy the definition of the floor function, floor(n t) is an integer and floor(n t) <= n t. Hence floor(n t) < m+1.\n\nBecause floor(n t) is an integer, the strict inequality floor(n t) < m+1 implies floor(n t) <= m. Therefore floor(n t) <= m, as required.",
